  "slug": "mutational-multimodal-36bc",
  "summary": "Recent preprints couple mutational signature inference, sequence-based mutation prediction and variant assessment across cancer and normal tissue.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the joint modelling of mutation-generating processes and variant consequences at sequence resolution. MOTET jointly infers multimodal mutational processes across SBS, DBS and indel signatures in 2,583 PCAWG cancer genomes. A monoallelic MUTYH carrier shows the oxidative SBS18 signature across multiple normal human tissues, suggesting a single hypomorphic allele. EAGLE-MUT, a DNA language model trained on cancer mutation data, predicts somatic mutation probabilities that vary with sequence context and nucleosome positioning. Two further preprints apply computational assessment to variant interpretation: base-editing accessibility of chromosome 21 ClinVar pathogenic variants with an African-ancestry focus, and integrated AlphaFold 3, stability and AlphaGenome analysis of TP63-IRF6-GRHL3 coding and non-coding variants. What is new is the combination of multiple mutation classes and predictive sequence models with variant-level assessment, although the items differ in scope and maturity.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
